{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 40, :comments ["<b>And the sojourning of the children of Israel, who dwelled in Egypt.</b> We find in the redemption from Egypt three appointed ends, and they are: 400 years, 430 years, 210 years. The 400 years are reckoned from the beginning of the sojourning of the seed of Avraham in affliction and poverty and the oppression of his children, as it is said (Genesis 15:13): ״ועבדום וענו אותם ארבע מאות שנה״ — and in this are included both sojourning and servitude. The 430 years are reckoned from Yitzhak, from the hour he was born. The 210 years — they dwelled in Egypt; the reckoning is 210. And likewise you will find in our future redemption three appointed ends in the book of Daniel: 1,150, 1,290, 1,335. This is as it is written first (Daniel 8:13): ״ויאמר אחד קדוש לפלמוני המדבר עד מתי החזון התמיד והפשע שומם תת וקדש וצבא מרמס״, and he said to me: ״עד ערב בקר אלפים ושלש מאות ונצדק קדש״ — reckon days and nights together, and since we divide them into days, they are 1,150 years; and it is written (Daniel 12:11): ״ומעת הוסר התמיד ולתת שקוץ שומם ימים אלף מאתים ותשעים״; and it is written after it (Daniel 12:12): ״אשרי המחכה ויגיע לימים אלף שלש מאות שלשים וחמשה״. And the cause was from the Holy One, blessed be He, that the redemption from Egypt was upon three appointed ends, in order to strengthen our slackened hands in this our exile, and that we not say our hope is lost — we have concluded for ourselves: since it has become clear to us that these three appointed ends of the redemption from Egypt were all of them true, so too must we believe that the three appointed ends specified in Daniel for our future redemption are all of them true, even though there are differing measures and reckonings there. Therefore three appointed ends were decreed both here and there: if they merit, they will be redeemed in the first; and if they do not merit in the first, they will merit in the second; and if they do not merit in the second, they will merit in the third; and it is impossible that the redemption be delayed beyond the last appointed end. Thus wrote Rabbenu Hananel, of blessed memory."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "The text quotes Daniel 8:14 as 'ונצדק קדש' rather than the standard 'ונצדק' of the Masoretic text, apparently following a variant reading."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:58:26.408484999Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}